{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"reasoning","text":"Let me now look at more schema files to understand the claim schemas and understand what's expected.\n\nLet me look at the specific tasks B8-017, B8-018, B8-024, B8-025 to understand what they need.\n\nGiven the context:\n- B8-017: SBOM/notices inventory shipped content & reconcile package contents\n- B8-018: Release manifest binds every referenced byte & verifier rejects missing/null/digest/signature/notarization/version/platform/equivalence/channel gaps\n- B8-024: Claims derive from exact qualification identity & downgrade mismatches\n- B8-025: Creator provenance verifies archive/output digests, category mappings, originality, shipped outputs, ownership, transformations, & writes qualification only when explicitly requested\n\nLet me trace through the acceptance criteria:\n\n1. **SBOM/notices inventory shipped content & reconcile package contents; unresolved rights block.**\n   - `sbom.mjs`: `reconcileDistributionContents()` checks shipped components against package contents. \n - Gap: It checks if `content.has(component.name)` - but `component.name` is the component name (like an npm package), while `packageContents` are file paths (like `bin/nemesis.mjs`). These don't match semantically. A shipped component \"lodash\" won't be found in a set of file paths. This is the **actual-state inventory** — it needs to map components to file paths, or check file digests, not just name matching.\n     - Also: `reconcileDistributionContents` only checks `!content.has(component.name)` but doesn't check if there's a content entry that maps to the component. The reconciliation should be path-based, not name-based. Actually, let me think about this more carefully...\n     \n   Actually wait, looking at this from the Book 8 perspective: the SBOM should reconcile the distribution contents against the shipped package contents. Currently `reconcileDistributionContents` checks shipped components' names against package content names/paths. But these are fundamentally different things — `component.name` is like \"npm-dep-name\" while `packageContents` are file paths like \"bin/nemesis.mjs\".\n\n   For an actual-state inventory, the reconciliation should check that every shipped component's file path (if it has one) is present in the package contents, not the component name. Let me look at `cyclonedxSbom` — it already uses `component.path` for file digests. So the `inventoryDistribution` function should include a `path` field, and `reconcileDistributionContents` should use `component.path` to match against package contents.\n\n   Actually, let me re-read more carefully. `inventoryDistribution` returns `{ type, name, version, source, digest, license, shipped, distributionStatus }` — no `path`. And `reconcileDistributionContents` checks `content.has(component.name)`.\n\n   Hmm, but the components in `inventoryDistribution` are things like external tools, grammars, rule material, creator material. For things that ship, their `name` should match a package content path. This might actually be correct by design if the `name` of a shipped component IS its path in the package.\n\n   Let me look at this differently. The task says \"actual-state inventories/reconciliation\". The current `reconcileDistributionContents` only filters shipped components and checks their name against package contents. But it doesn't verify that non-shipped components that SHOULD be there are actually there. Also, it doesn't check digests of content files against component digests.\n\n   Actually, I think the main gap for B8-017 is:\n   - `reconcileDistributionContents` only checks `!content.has(component.name)` but should ALSO verify that for components with `digest`, the actual file digest matches. Currently, it checks metadata completeness (digest, license, source) but NOT that the actual byte content matches what the component declares.\n   \n   Wait, actually it does check `!component.digest` — that checks that the digest field exists. But it doesn't check that the actual file on disk matches the declared digest. That's a gap.\n\n   Let me re-read the acceptance criteria more carefully: \"SBOM/notices inventory shipped content & reconcile package contents\". So the SBOM should inventory what's actually shipped, and the reconciliation should verify the contents match.\n\n   The `reconcileDistributionContents` function checks:\n   1. `content.has(component.name)` — component is in package   2. `component.digest`, `component.license`, `component.source` — metadata present   Missing: \n   - Verification that when a component has a `path` field, the actual file exists and digest matches\n   - Verification of content-level integrity   Actually, let me re-focus. The current code already does the basic check. Let me look for actual gaps more carefully.\n\n2. **Release manifest binds every referenced byte & verifier rejects missing/null/digest/signature/notarization/version/platform/equivalence/channel gaps.**\n   \n   Looking at `verify-release.mjs`:\n   - Checks for missing checksums, SBOMs, notices, signatures, notarization, qualification artifacts ✓\n   - Checks artifact digests ✓\n   - Checks SBOM content is non-empty ✓\n   - Checks signature status is not placeholder/missing ✓\n   - Checks platform is present for native artifacts ✓\n   - Checks semantic equivalence ✓\n   - Checks channels have artifact decisions ✓\n   - Checks version and sourceRevision ✓\n\n   Gap: The verifier checks `!(manifest[field] ?? []).length` for required sections, but it doesn't check that each required section (checksums, sboms, notices, signatures, notarization, qualificationArtifacts) has at least one entry. Wait, it does: `if (!(manifest[field] ?? []).length) issues.push(...)`.\n\n   But: The verifier doesn't check `signature.status` exists — only checks if status is 'placeholder' or 'missing'. A null status would pass. Also doesn't check `notarization` entries for completeness.\n\n   Wait, looking more carefully: `if (!signature.digest || ['placeholder', 'missing'].includes(signature.status))` — this checks digest exists and status is not placeholder/missing. But if `signature.status` is null, it would NOT be in the array, so null status passes. That's a gap.\n\n   Also: The channels check only checks artifact count matches, not that each channel has the correct decision for each artifact. But it does check `channel.artifacts` length vs `manifest.artifacts` length. If a channel has extra entries, that's not checked. But the core issue is: `channel.artifacts` length must equal `manifest.artifacts` length.\n\n   Actually, looking at this more carefully: The channel check `if ((channel.artifacts ?? []).length !== (manifest.artifacts ?? []).length)` is correct for length. But it doesn't verify that each artifact path in the channel matches an artifact in the manifest. That could be a gap, but it's minor.\n\n   Let me focus on the most impactful gaps:\n   - `signature.status` null/undefined passes silently\n   - `notarization` entries are not validated for completeness (no digest/status checks)\n   - `qualificationArtifacts` entries are not validated (only existence/digest check)\n\n3. **Claims derive from exact qualification identity & downgrade mismatches.**\n\n   Looking at `claims.mjs`:\n   - `generateClaims` checks that identity fields match expected identity ✓\n   - If any field doesn't match, state is downgraded to 'unproven' ✓\n   - `generateClaimsFromQualification` passes `expectedIdentity` to each claim ✓\n\n   Gap: `generateClaims` creates identity from `item.artifactDigest`, `item.corpusDigest`, `item.providerDigest`. If any of these are `null`, the `qualified` check `identity.artifactDigest && identity.corpusDigest && identity.providerDigest` will be false, and state will be downgraded to 'unproven'. That's correct.\n\n   But: The `limitations` default message is `'qualification identity incomplete'` — this is a single message regardless of which field is missing. Not necessarily a gap but could be more specific.\n\n   Actually, looking at the `limitations` field: when `qualified` is false, `limitations` defaults to `['qualification identity incomplete']` ONLY if `item.limitations` is not set. But there's no check that the limitations are semantically correct for the type of mismatch. This is minor.\n\n   Let me check if `limitations` is properly set when identity mismatch occurs but `item.limitations` is also provided. If `item.limitations` is provided, it overrides the default, even if the identity mismatch is different. This could hide the actual reason for downgrade. But that's by design — the qualification provides its own limitations.\n\n   I think the main gap here is: The identity comparison uses `Object.keys(identity).every(key => identity[key] === expected[key])`. But what if `expected` has additional keys that aren't in `identity`? The comparison only checks that all keys in `identity` match `expected`, not that `expected` is an exact match. However, `identity` is constructed from exactly three fields, so this should be fine.\n\n   Actually wait — there's a subtle issue. `expected` is `item.expectedIdentity ?? null`. If `expected` is null, then `qualified` is false (because `expected && ...` short-circuits). But then the `Object.keys(identity).every(...)` would also work correctly with null since it's short-circuited. So this is fine.\n\n   Hmm, but what about this: if `expected` is `{ artifactDigest: 'a', corpusDigest: 'b', providerDigest: 'c', extraKey: 'd' }`, then the check `Object.keys(identity).every(key => identity[key] === expected[key])` would pass because `identity` only has 3 keys and all 3 match. The extra key in `expected` is ignored. This means the qualification could have a different expected identity shape and still qualify. Is that a gap? The acceptance criteria says \"derive from exact qualification identity\" — this suggests the identity must be an exact match, not just a subset.\n\n   YES — this is a gap. The check should verify that `expected` has exactly the same keys as `identity`, not just that all `identity` keys are in `expected`. Otherwise, you could pass with an `expected` that has fewer fields than required.\n\n   Actually wait, `identity` has exactly 3 keys (artifactDigest, corpusDigest, providerDigest), and `expected` must have those 3 to match. If `expected` has fewer keys, the comparison would fail because some `identity` keys wouldn't be in `expected` (returning `undefined` which !== the actual value). If `expected` has more keys, they'd be ignored. So the issue is only with extra keys.\n\n   For \"exact\" identity, extra keys in `expected` that don't exist in `identity` should cause a mismatch. Let me fix this.\n\n4. **Creator provenance verifies archive/output digests, category mappings, originality, shipped outputs, ownership, transformations, & writes qualification only when explicitly requested.**\n\n   Looking at `verify-source-provenance.mjs`:\n   - Checks digest format ✓\n   - Checks outputDigest format ✓\n   - Checks redistribution grant for shipped ✓\n   - Checks outputDigest exists ✓\n   - Checks transformations array exists and non-empty ✓\n   - Checks prompt prose not shipped ✓\n   - Checks ownership for designer/writing ✓\n   - Checks categoryMappings, originalRuleOwnership, shippedOutputs ✓\n   - Checks file digests ✓\n\n   Gap in `writeSourceProvenanceQualification`: It always writes the qualification result, regardless of whether the user requested it. The acceptance criteria says \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested.\" Currently, `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` writes unconditionally.\n\n   Actually, looking at the code: `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` takes `outputPath` and writes. But the CLI main block only writes if `--out` is provided: `if (outIndex >= 0) writeFileSync(...)`. So the CLI is correct — it only writes when `--out` is specified. But the exported function `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` always writes. \n\n   Wait, the acceptance criteria says \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested.\" The `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` function always writes to the output path. This is by design — it's called \"write\" in the name. But the CLI only calls it when `--out` is specified. Actually wait, the CLI doesn't call `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` at all — it calls `verifySourceProvenance` and then manually writes with `writeFileSync`. So `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` is never called from the CLI.\n\n   Hmm, but the exported function `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` should perhaps guard against writing when the decision is BLOCKED. The acceptance criteria says \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested\" — this could mean it should not write when the result is BLOCKED (since that's not a qualification), or it could mean the function should only write when the caller explicitly opts in.\n\n   I think the intent is: don't write a qualification file when the source provenance is BLOCKED. Writing a BLOCKED result to a qualification file would be misleading. So `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` should only write when the decision is QUALIFIED, or at minimum, it should not write if `result.decision !== 'QUALIFIED'`.\n\n   Actually, re-reading the acceptance criteria: \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested.\" I think this means the function should not auto-write; it should only write when a specific flag is set. But the function name already implies \"write\".   Let me re-read: \"Creator provenance verifies archive/output digests, category mappings, originality, shipped outputs, ownership, transformations, & writes qualification only when explicitly requested.\"\n\n   I think \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested\" means the `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` function should check an explicit opt-in parameter before writing. Like a `force` or `write` parameter. Without it, it should return the result without writing.\n\n   Actually, looking at the current code, `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` ALWAYS writes to the output path. The acceptance criteria says it should only write when explicitly requested. So we need to add an explicit guard.\n\n   But wait — looking at the CLI code:   ```\n   if (outIndex >= 0) writeFileSync(resolve(process.argv[outIndex + 1]), `${JSON.stringify(result, null, 2)}\\n`, 'utf8');\n   ```\n   The CLI only writes when `--out` is specified. The `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` function is not used by the CLI. But the exported function is available for programmatic use. Should the function require an explicit opt-in?\n\n   I think the correct interpretation is: the `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` function should NOT write when `result.decision !== 'QUALIFIED'`. A BLOCKED provenance should not be written as a \"qualification\". This aligns with the \"fail-closed\" principle and \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested\" — writing a BLOCKED result is not writing a qualification.\n\n   Let me now compile all the gaps I've found:\n\n**B8-017 Gaps (SBOM/Notices):**\n1. `reconcileDistributionContents` doesn't verify actual file digests match component digests for shipped components that have paths. But wait — `inventoryDistribution` doesn't include `path`. So the reconciliation can't check file digests. The gap is that `reconcileDistributionContents` checks `component.digest` exists but doesn't verify it against the actual file.\n   \n   Actually, I need to look at this differently. The components in `inventoryDistribution` may or may not have paths. The function merges runtime, externalTools, grammars, ruleMaterial, creatorMaterial — none of which have `path` in the function. So `component.path` is always undefined in the output. But the `cyclonedxSbom` function does use `component.path`.\n\n   Wait, looking at `inventoryDistribution`:\n   ```js\n   .map((component) => ({\n     type: component.type ?? 'library', name: component.name, version: component.version ?? null,\n     source: component.source ?? null, digest: component.digest ?? null, license: component.license ?? null,\n     shipped: component.shipped === true, distributionStatus: component.distributionStatus ?? 'reference-only',\n   }))\n   ```\n   It doesn't pass through `path`. So even if input components have `path`, it's dropped. This means the reconciliation can never verify file-level integrity.\n\n   For the reconciliation to be meaningful, `inventoryDistribution` should pass through `path` when present.\n\n   Also, `reconcileDistributionContents` should verify that if a component has a `path`, the file exists and its digest matches.\n\n2. The `reconcileDistributionContents` currently checks `content.has(component.name)` which compares a component name (like \"lodash\") against package content paths (like \"bin/nemesis.mjs\"). For shipped components, this makes sense only if the component name IS the content path. But for runtime dependencies (npm packages), the component name is the package name, not the file path. So this check may give false positives or negatives.\n\n   Actually, I think for the reconciliation purpose, we need two levels:\n   - For components that have a `path`: check that path is in package contents and digest matches\n   - For components without a `path` (like npm deps): check that metadata is complete\n\n   But currently, `inventoryDistribution` drops the `path`. Let me fix this.\n\nOK let me step back and think about what's really needed to close the acceptance gaps with minimal changes. Let me focus on the most impactful, clearly-required gaps:\n\n### Key Gaps to Fix:\n\n**sbom.mjs:**\n1. `inventoryDistribution` drops `path` — should preserve it for reconciliation\n2. `reconcileDistributionContents` only checks name-in-set and metadata completeness, but doesn't verify actual file digest when path is available\n\n**verify-release.mjs:**\n3. Signature null status passes (only checks string equality 'placeholder'/'missing')\n4. Notarization entries not validated for digest/status\n5. No check that signature status field exists (null/undefined bypasses the check)\n\n**claims.mjs:**\n6. Identity comparison allows extra keys in expected — should be exact match (key count must match)\n\n**verify-source-provenance.mjs:**\n7. `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` always writes — should only write when decision is QUALIFIED (writes qualification only when explicitly requested / when it is actually a qualification)\n\n**creator-skills.json:**\n8. Missing `digest` pattern fix? No, the digests are placeholder values but that's expected since the actual archives don't exist yet.\n\nLet me count the changes:\n- sbom.mjs: ~10 lines change\n- verify-release.mjs: ~8 lines change\n- claims.mjs: ~3 lines change\n- verify-source-provenance.mjs: ~3 lines change\n- Total: ~24 lines — well within 180\n\nLet me now draft the patches.\n\nActually, let me re-examine each more carefully before patching.\n\nFor **sbom.mjs**:\n1. `inventoryDistribution` should preserve `path`:\n```js\nexport function inventoryDistribution({ runtime = [], externalTools = [], grammars = [], ruleMaterial = [], creatorMaterial = [] } = {}) {\n  return [...runtime, ...externalTools, ...grammars, ...ruleMaterial, ...creatorMaterial]\n    .map((component) => ({\n      type: component.type ?? 'library', name: component.name, version: component.version ?? null,\n      source: component.source ?? null, path: component.path ?? null, digest: component.digest ?? null, license: component.license ?? null,\n      shipped: component.shipped === true, distributionStatus: component.distributionStatus ?? 'reference-only',\n    }))\n    .sort((a, b) => `${a.type}:${a.name}`.localeCompare(`${b.type}:${b.name}`));\n}\n```\n\n2. `reconcileDistributionContents` should verify file digest when path is available:\n```js\nexport function reconcileDistributionContents(components = [], packageContents = [], { root = null } = {}) {\n  const shipped = components.filter(({ shipped }) => shipped);\n  const content = new Set(packageContents.map((entry) => typeof entry === 'string' ? entry : entry.name));\n  const blockers = shipped.flatMap((component) => {\n    const missing = [];\n    if (component.path) {\n      if (!content.has(component.path)) missing.push({ kind: 'shipped-component-absent', component: component.name });\n      if (root && component.digest && fileDigest(resolve(root, component.path)) !== component.digest) missing.push({ kind: 'component-digest-mismatch', component: component.name });\n    } else {\n      if (!content.has(component.name)) missing.push({ kind: 'shipped-component-absent', component: component.name });\n    }\n    if (!component.digest || !component.license || !component.source) missing.push({ kind: 'component-metadata-incomplete', component: component.name });\n    return missing;\n  });\n  return { components: shipped, blockers, decision: blockers.length ? 'BLOCKED' : 'QUALIFIED' };\n}\n```\n\nWait, but this changes the function signature. The existing callers might not pass `root`. Let me check if there are callers...\n\nThe function is exported, and the acceptance criteria says no tests. Let me keep it backward-compatible with an optional options parameter.\n\nActually, let me also import `resolve` from node:path in sbom.mjs since it uses `fileDigest` from release-manifest.mjs which already does path resolution. Wait, `fileDigest` already resolves internally? Let me check:\n\n```js\nexport function fileDigest(path) {\n  return existsSync(path) ? sha256(readFileSync(path)) : null;\n}\n```\n\nNo, `fileDigest` takes a path and checks if it exists as-is. It doesn't resolve relative to root. So I'd need to resolve first.\n\nActually, let me simplify. The `reconcileDistributionContents` function should be enhanced but the key gap is:\n1. If component has `path`, check path is in packageContents, not just name\n2. If component has `path` and `root`, verify digestLet me keep it simpler — just fix the path matching and the `inventoryDistribution` path preservation.\n\nFor **verify-release.mjs**:\n3. Fix null/undefined signature status:\n```js\nfor (const signature of manifest.signatures ?? []) if (!signature.digest || !signature.status || ['placeholder', 'missing'].includes(signature.status)) issues.push(...)\n```\nChange: add `!signature.status` check before the includes check.\n\n4. Validate notarization entries:\n```js\nfor (const entry of manifest.notarization ?? []) {\n  if (!entry.digest) issues.push({ artifact: entry.path ?? 'notarization', issue: 'notarization-digest-missing' });\n  if (!entry.status || ['placeholder', 'missing'].includes(entry.status)) issues.push({ artifact: entry.path ?? 'notarization', issue: 'notarization-status-invalid' });\n}\n```\n\nActually wait, looking at the existing code again:\n```js\nfor (const signature of manifest.signatures ?? []) if (!signature.digest || ['placeholder', 'missing'].includes(signature.status)) issues.push({ artifact: signature.path ?? 'signature', issue: 'placeholder-signature' });\n```\n\nThe issue: `signature.status` could be `null` or `undefined`, and `['placeholder', 'missing'].includes(null)` is `false`, so null status passes. The fix is to add `!signature.status`.\n\nFor **claims.mjs**:\n5. Exact identity match — check that expected has no extra keys:\n```js\nconst qualified = Boolean(expected && identity.artifactDigest && identity.corpusDigest && identity.providerDigest \n  && Object.keys(identity).every((key) => identity[key] === expected[key])\n  && Object.keys(expected).length === Object.keys(identity).length);\n```\n\nFor **verify-source-provenance.mjs**:\n6. `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` should only write when decision is QUALIFIED:\n```js\nexport function writeSourceProvenanceQualification(manifest, outputPath, options = {}) {\n  if (!outputPath) throw new Error('outputPath is required');\n  const result = verifySourceProvenance(manifest, options);\n  if (result.decision !== 'QUALIFIED') return result;\n  writeFileSync(resolve(outputPath), `${JSON.stringify(result, null, 2)}\\n`, 'utf8');\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nNow let me count total changed lines more carefully and then apply the patches.\n\nLet me re-examine the exact lines I need to change:\n\n**sbom.mjs:**\n- Line ~31: Add `path: component.path ?? null` to inventoryDistribution output mapping (+1 line modified)\n- Lines ~37-45: Update reconcileDistributionContents to check path-based matching and optionally verify digest (+~6 lines modified)\n\n**verify-release.mjs:**\n- Line ~69: Add `!signature.status` check (+1 small change)\n- Lines ~71-72 area: Add notarization entry validation (+2 lines)\n\n**claims.mjs:**\n- Line ~11: Add `Object.keys(expected).length === Object.keys(identity).length` check (+1 line)\n\n**verify-source-provenance.mjs:**\n- Line ~31-33: Add `if (result.decision !== 'QUALIFIED') return result;` guard (+2 lines)\n\nTotal: ~12-15 changed lines. Well within 180.\n\nLet me apply these patches now.\n\nWait, one more thing — let me also check if there are any issues with the `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` and `claims.json`/`support.md` files. These are generated data files. Do they need updates?\n\n- `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`: The acceptance criteria says \"SBOM/notices inventory shipped content & reconcile package contents; unresolved rights block.\" The notices file already says it tracks actual dependencies. It looks complete for the current state (no external dependencies). I don't think it needs changes.\n\n- `references/generated/claims.json`: Shows empty claims with known gaps. This is correct — the qualification identity is absent. No change needed.\n\n- `references/generated/support.md`: Says no public support claim is qualified. Correct for current state. No change needed.\n\n- `references/source-provenance/creator-skills.json`: The creator provenance manifest with known gaps. This is correct as-is — the digests are placeholders because the actual archives don't exist. No change needed.\n\nOK, let me apply the patches now. Let me be very precise about the line numbers and exact changes."}}
