{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"reasoning","text":"Let me analyze the acceptance criteria against the current code to find the remaining gaps.\n\n**Task B8-017: SBOM/notices inventory shipped content & reconcile package contents; unresolved rights block**\n\nLooking at `sbom.mjs`:\n- `reconcileDistributionContents` - checks shipped components against package contents. Currently it checks `content.has(component.name)` which compares against the set of package content entries. But package contents are file paths (strings), not component names. A component named \"foo\" won't be found in a set of paths like `[\"lib/foo.mjs\", \"bin/foo.mjs\"]`. The reconciliation is name-based not path-based. This is a gap: shipped components should match by path prefix, not exact name.\n- Also `reconcileDistributionContents` doesn't check `version` in the metadata completeness check.\n\nLooking at `notices.mjs`:\n- `buildNoticeInventory` - checks `redistributionGrant` for external sources. This seems reasonable.\n- But there's no reconciliation against actual THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md content. The acceptance says \"SBOM/notices inventory shipped content & reconcile package contents\" - notices should also reconcile their rendered output against the actual THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.\n\nLooking at `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` - it's a static file with sections. The notices module renders components but doesn't reconcile against the actual file. However, this is about the code logic being correct for when it's called.\n\nLet me think more carefully about what \"reconcile package contents\" means here. The `package.json` has a `files` array which is the npm published package contents. The `MANIFEST.package.json` has `allowlistedTopLevel`. The reconciliation should check shipped components against the published file list.\n\nThe current `reconcileDistributionContents` takes `packageContents` as an argument, but the comparison `content.has(component.name)` is wrong - component names like \"nemesis\" don't appear in file paths like \"lib/index.mjs\". This is a real bug.\n\nActually wait - looking more carefully, the `components` parameter here is the output of `inventoryDistribution` or `inventoryRuntimeDependencies`. Those have names like package names or component names. The `packageContents` would be the file listing. Comparing a name like \"nemesis\" against a file path like \"bin/nemesis.mjs\" won't work.\n\nThe reconciliation should probably check that for each shipped component, there exists at least one package content entry that contains the component name or path. Let me think about this...\n\nActually, for runtime dependencies (npm packages), the component name IS the package name. For other component types (externalTools, grammars, ruleMaterial, creatorMaterial), the component might have a `path` field. The reconciliation should match components to package contents by path when available, and by name when that's the identifier.\n\nBut actually, looking at the acceptance criteria again: \"SBOM/notices inventory shipped content & reconcile package contents; unresolved rights block.\" This means:\n1. SBOM and notices should inventory what's actually shipped\n2. Reconcile against the package contents (the file list)\n3. Unresolved redistribution rights should block\n\nThe key gap in `reconcileDistributionContents` is that it compares component name directly against the content set, which will fail because package contents are paths. It should do a path-based or prefix-based match.\n\nFor notices: `buildNoticeInventory` checks redistribution rights, which is good. But it doesn't have any reconciliation with the actual shipped notices content (THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md). However, maybe reconciliation of notices against the md file isn't what's being asked - the notices code just needs to produce correct inventory and block when rights are unresolved.\n\nLet me also check: does `reconcileDistributionContents` correctly handle the \"unresolved rights block\" part? Looking at the blockers it checks: `shipped-component-absent` and `component-metadata-incomplete` (missing digest, license, or source). It doesn't check redistribution rights. But `buildNoticeInventory` does check redistribution rights. These are two separate functions serving different purposes.\n\nActually wait - `reconcileDistributionContents` is in `sbom.mjs` and `buildNoticeInventory` is in `notices.mjs`. The SBOM reconciliation should probably also check redistribution rights for completeness. But looking at the existing code, `buildNoticeInventory` already handles the rights check separately. The `reconcileDistributionContents` function focuses on content presence and metadata completeness. The rights check is a separate concern handled by notices.\n\nHmm, but the acceptance criteria says \"unresolved rights block\" as part of the SBOM/notices inventory. I think the issue is that `reconcileDistributionContents` should also block when a shipped component has no redistribution grant and is from an external source. Let me check the component shape... Components from `inventoryDistribution` have a `source` field but no `redistributionGrant` field. The `source` could be 'external' etc.\n\nActually, I think the real gap is simpler. Let me re-read the acceptance criteria:\n- \"SBOM/notices inventory shipped content & reconcile package contents; unresolved rights block.\"\n\nThis means: the SBOM and notices functions should (1) inventory shipped content correctly, (2) reconcile against package contents, and (3) unresolved redistribution rights should produce blockers.\n\nThe `reconcileDistributionContents` doesn't check redistribution rights at all. But this function receives components from `inventoryDistribution` which don't have a `redistributionGrant` field. The redistribution grant checking is in `buildNoticeInventory` which receives components with that field.\n\nLooking at the actual code flow, I think the gap in `reconcileDistributionContents` is:\n1. Name-based matching against package contents is wrong (should handle paths)\n2. Missing `version` completeness check (only checks digest, license, source)\n\n**Task B8-018: Release manifest binds every referenced byte & verifier rejects missing/null/digest/signature/notarization/version/platform/equivalence/channel gaps**\n\nLooking at `release-manifest.mjs`:\n- `buildReleaseManifest` - creates the manifest. It binds artifacts with digests. But it doesn't validate that signatures, notarization, etc. are present. The builder function should enforce that all binding references are present and non-null.\n\nLooking at `verify-release.mjs`:\n- Already checks for missing/null/digest/signature/notarization/version/platform/equivalence/channel gaps\n- But there are gaps:\n1. The `verifyReferenced` function doesn't check notarization entries for specific required fields like `status`\n  2. Channel artifacts check: `channel.artifacts.length !== manifest.artifacts.length` - this checks count but not that each artifact path is represented. A channel could have different artifacts with the same count.\n  3. The verifier doesn't check that `signatures` entries have a `status` field that isn't null\n  4. Missing null/artifact.path validation in verifyReferenced - if record.path is null/undefined, the current code would create a resolvedArtifact with null path which would return null, and then push a 'missing' issue. That seems OK.\n\nLet me look more carefully:\n\nIn `verifyReferenced`:\n```js\nfunction verifyReferenced(root, entries, type, issues) {\n  for (const entry of entries ?? []) {\n    const record = typeof entry === 'string' ? { path: entry } : entry;\n    const path = resolvedArtifact(root, record.path);\n    if (!path || !existsSync(path)) issues.push({ artifact: record.path ?? type, issue: 'missing' });\n    else if (!record.digest) issues.push({ artifact: record.path, issue: 'digest-missing' });\n    else if (sha256File(path) !== record.digest) issues.push({ artifact: record.path, issue: 'digest-mismatch' });\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis doesn't check signature status or notarization status. For signatures specifically, there's a separate check:\n```js\nfor (const signature of manifest.signatures ?? []) if (!signature.digest || ['placeholder', 'missing'].includes(signature.status)) issues.push(...)\n```\n\nBut it doesn't check if `signature.status` is null or missing. If `signature.status` is undefined (not in the record), the `['placeholder', 'missing'].includes(undefined)` is false, so it wouldn't be caught. Similarly if `signature.status` is null, `includes(null)` is false.\n\nFor notarization: there's no status check at all in the verifier. The acceptance criteria says \"notarization gaps\" should be rejected.\n\nFor version check: the verifier checks `!manifest.version` but doesn't check individual artifact versions.\n\nFor platform check: it checks `artifact.type === 'native' && !artifact.platform` but doesn't check that platform is a valid value.\n\nFor equivalence: checks `artifact.semanticEquivalent === false` but not that semantic equivalent entries are non-null.\n\nFor channels: checks count but not path-level matching.\n\nLet me also check what fields the `buildReleaseManifest` requires from channels. The builder takes `channels` with `decision` and `artifactDecisions`. The built channel has per-artifact decisions. But the verifier only checks count equality, not path-level matching.\n\n**Task B8-024: Claims derive from exact qualification identity & downgrade mismatches**\n\nLooking at `claims.mjs`:\n- `generateClaims` checks `expectedIdentity` matches `identity` field by field. This is correct.\n- `generateClaimsFromQualification` passes `expectedIdentity` to each record.\n- But the current `generateClaims` uses `Object.keys(identity).every(...)` which only checks keys present in `identity`, not all keys in `expected`. If expected has extra keys that identity doesn't have, those aren't checked. Wait no - `identity` is `{ artifactDigest, corpusDigest, providerDigest }` so all keys come from the qualification item. But the identity comparison only checks `Object.keys(identity)` - meaning it checks all three keys exist and match. That seems correct.\n\nActually, let me re-read: `Object.keys(identity).every((key) => identity[key] === expected[key])` - this checks that for every key in `identity`, the value matches `expected`. But what if `expected` has keys that `identity` doesn't? Those wouldn't be checked. However, since both `identity` and `expected` are constructed from the same shape (`{ artifactDigest, corpusDigest, providerDigest }`), this should be fine.\n\nBut wait - the acceptance says \"Claims derive from exact qualification identity & downgrade mismatches.\" The \"downgrade mismatch\" part means: if the qualification identity doesn't match exactly, the claim should downgrade to 'unproven' (not keep the original state). Let me check...\n\n```js\nstate: qualified ? state : 'unproven',\n```\n\nYes, it downgrades to 'unproven'. But `STATES` includes 'unavailable'. Should downgraded claims use 'unavailable' instead of 'unproven'? The acceptance says \"downgrade mismatches\" which could mean the state should be downgraded. Currently it goes to 'unproven' which is one of the valid states. I think this is actually correct - 'unproven' means \"we can't claim this is proven because the identity doesn't match.\"\n\nBut actually, there's another gap: when `expectedIdentity` is null, the `qualified` boolean is false, so all claims get 'unproven'. But the limitations should specifically mention the identity mismatch. Currently:\n```js\nlimitations: item.limitations ?? (qualified ? [] : ['qualification identity incomplete']),\n```\n\nIf the item already has `limitations` set, it keeps them even if the identity doesn't match. This means a claim could have custom limitations that don't mention the identity mismatch. The acceptance says claims should \"derive from exact qualification identity & downgrade mismatches\" - this implies when there's a mismatch, the limitation should explicitly mention it.\n\nWait, but `item.limitations` could be explicitly set by the caller. If a caller provides limitations, we should still include the identity mismatch limitation. So the fix would be to always prepend or append the identity mismatch limitation when the identity doesn't match, regardless of what `item.limitations` says.\n\nActually, let me re-read: \"downgrade mismatches\" - I think this means when the identity doesn't match exactly, the claim should be downgraded. The current code does this by setting state to 'unproven'. But there's a subtlety: if `expected` is null but all three digests are present, `qualified` is still false. This is correct because without an expected identity to compare against, we can't confirm qualification.\n\nLet me also look at the claims.json output:\n```json\n{\n  \"qualificationIdentity\": null,\n  \"claims\": [],\n  \"knownGaps\": [\"final qualification, provider, corpus, skill, and artifact digests are absent\"]\n}\n```\n\nThe `knownGaps` message is hardcoded, not derived from the actual data. But this is generated output, not code. The code is `generateClaimsFromQualification` which checks `expectedIdentity ? [] : ['exact qualification identity is absent']`.\n\nThe claims.json has a different gap message which appears to be a manually crafted file. That's fine.\n\n**Task B8-025: Creator provenance verifies archive/output digests, category mappings, originality, shipped outputs, ownership, transformations, & writes qualification only when explicitly requested**\n\nLooking at `verify-source-provenance.mjs`:\n- Checks for digest pattern, outputDigest, redistributionGrant, transformations, promptProseShipped, ownership, categoryMappings, originalRuleOwnership, shippedOutputs\n- `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` writes qualification only when called with an outputPath\n\nBut there are gaps:\n1. The `verifySourceProvenance` function checks `source.redistributionGrant !== true` for shipped sources, but it doesn't check that `source.redistributionGrant` is explicitly `true` (vs truthy). Actually, `!== true` is strict, so `1` would also block. That seems correct.\n\n2. For the digest pattern check: `!digestPattern.test(source.digest ?? '') || !digestPattern.test(source.outputDigest ?? '')` - if outputDigest is undefined/null, it defaults to '' which fails the test. This catches missing outputDigest. Good.\n\n3. But the check `if (!source.outputDigest) blockers.push(...)` is redundant with the pattern check (since '' would fail pattern). Not a bug, just redundant.\n\n4. The `transformations` check requires a non-empty array, which is good.\n\n5. For \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested\" - the `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` function requires `outputPath` and only writes when called. The CLI only writes when `--out` is provided. This seems correct.\n\n6. But wait - `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` doesn't check if the result is QUALIFIED before writing. It writes regardless. The acceptance says \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested\" - this might mean it should only write when the result is QUALIFIED, or it might mean it should only write when an outputPath is provided. Currently it writes whenever outputPath is provided, regardless of decision. Let me think... \"writes qualification\" probably means \"writes the qualification result file\" which should only happen when explicitly requested via `--out` or `outputPath`. This is already the case. But maybe it should only write when QUALIFIED? That would make it write BLOCKED results too, which seems fine for audit purposes.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested\" - I think this means the function should not have any side effects unless the caller explicitly asks for a write. Currently `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` only writes when called with outputPath, which is explicit. The CLI only writes when `--out` is present. This seems correct.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue: in the CLI section:\n```js\nconst result = existsSync(input) ? verifySourceProvenance(JSON.parse(readFileSync(input, 'utf8'))) : verifySourceProvenance(null);\n```\n\nWhen `verifySourceProvenance(null)` is called, `sources` would be `manifest?.sources ?? []` which is `[]` since null?.sources is undefined. Then the empty sources check would add `source-provenance-absent` blocker. This is correct.\n\nNow let me look at what `creator-skills.json` actually contains and whether the verification would catch all the gaps...\n\nThe creator-skills.json has sources with:\n- digest: \"missing:creator-archive\" (not sha256 format)\n- outputDigest: \"missing:final-transformation-manifest\" (not sha256 format)\n- categoryMappings: [] (empty)\n- originalRuleOwnership: \"unverified\" (not \"repository-original\")\n- shippedOutputs: [] (empty)\n\nThe verifier checks:\n- Digest pattern: would fail for both digest and outputDigest ✓\n- outputDigest presence: would catch missing ✓\n- Transformations: has non-empty array ✓\n- CategoryMappings: empty array would fail ✓ (wait, let me check... `!Array.isArray(source.categoryMappings) || !source.categoryMappings.length` - empty array has length 0, so `!0` is true, so this would add a blocker ✓)\n- originalRuleOwnership: \"unverified\" !== \"repository-original\" ✓\n- shippedOutputs: empty array - `!Array.isArray(source.shippedOutputs)` is false, but there's no check for `!source.shippedOutputs.length`. Actually wait, let me re-read: `!Array.isArray(source.categoryMappings) || !source.categoryMappings.length || source.originalRuleOwnership !== 'repository-original' || !Array.isArray(source.shippedOutputs)` - this checks that shippedOutputs IS an array, but doesn't check it's non-empty.Wait, actually the condition is: if ANY of these is true, add blocker. So:\n- `!source.categoryMappings.length` → true (empty array) → blocker added ✓\n\nThe `shippedOutputs` check only verifies it's an array, not that it's non-empty. The acceptance says the verifier should check \"shipped outputs\" - this probably means shipped outputs should be non-empty if there are shipped outputs, or at least be present.\n\nActually, looking at the combined condition more carefully:\n```js\nif (!Array.isArray(source.categoryMappings) || !source.categoryMappings.length || source.originalRuleOwnership !== 'repository-original' || !Array.isArray(source.shippedOutputs))\n```\n\nThis is a single OR condition. If categoryMappings is empty (length 0), the whole condition is true and the blocker is added. So the shippedOutputs check is never reached. But if categoryMappings were non-empty and originalRuleOwnership was correct, then shippedOutputs would only be checked for being an array, not non-empty.\n\nThis is a gap: shippedOutputs should be checked for non-emptiness.\n\nAlso, the function doesn't verify archive digests specifically. The acceptance says \"verifies archive/output digests\" - the current code checks that digest and outputDigest are present and match sha256 pattern, but doesn't verify that they correspond to actual files. However, the `verifyFiles` option does this when paths are provided.\n\nLet me now think about what the smallest corrections are to close the gaps:\n\n**B8-017 gaps:**\n1. `reconcileDistributionContents` compares component.name against packageContents set, but packageContents entries are paths. Need to match by path prefix or by checking if the component name appears as a package in the file tree. The fix: for each shipped component, check if any package content entry starts with the component's path or name.\n2. Missing `version` in completeness check (only checks digest, license, source).\n\nActually wait - for runtime dependencies, the component name IS a package name like \"lodash\". The packageContents would be file paths. \"lodash\" wouldn't be found in a set of file paths. But npm dependencies aren't directly in the file list - they're in node_modules/. So the reconciliation for runtime deps against file paths doesn't make much sense. \n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Maybe `packageContents` is meant to be a list of package names/identifiers, not file paths. If it's the npm package list, then names would match. But looking at the code, it handles both strings and objects with `.name`:\n```js\nconst content = new Set(packageContents.map((entry) => typeof entry === 'string' ? entry : entry.name));\n```\n\nThis normalizes to a set of strings. If packageContents is an array of path strings from npm, names wouldn't match. If it's an array of {name: \"foo\"} objects, names would match.\n\nI think the real issue is that the function should also match components by their `path` field if available, or check if the component name is a substring of any package content path.\n\nActually, let me look at this differently. The `inventoryDistribution` function creates components from categories. Each component has a `name` field. The `reconcileDistributionContents` is supposed to verify that every shipped component actually appears in the package contents. The current code only does exact name matching.\n\nFor a correct reconciliation, shipped components should match against package contents by:\n1. Exact name match (for dependencies)\n2. Path prefix match (for files/directories)\n\nLet me look at what `reconcileDistributionContents` is actually called with in practice... I don't see calls to it in the codebase from the files I've read. It's likely called from build/release scripts. \n\nGiven the constraints (max 180 changed lines, smallest corrections), I should make targeted fixes:\n\n1. **sbom.mjs `reconcileDistributionContents`**: Change name-only matching to also check path-based containment. Add version to completeness check.\n2. **release-manifest.mjs `buildReleaseManifest`**: Add validation that all referenced entries have required fields (version, platform for native, etc.)\n3. **verify-release.mjs**: Fix channel artifact path matching (check paths, not just count), add signature status null/missing check, add notarization status check, add null version/artifact checks4. **claims.mjs**: Ensure identity mismatch limitations are always appended (not just when no limitations provided)\n5. **verify-source-provenance.mjs**: Add shippedOutputs non-emptiness check, ensure archive digest verification\n\nLet me also think about the `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` reconciliation. The notices module `buildNoticeInventory` produces an inventory but doesn't reconcile against the actual `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` content. Should it? The acceptance says \"SBOM/notices inventory shipped content & reconcile package contents\". I think \"reconcile package contents\" means reconcile against the package's file listing, not against the notices file itself. The notices file IS a package content.\n\nOK, let me also look at the `verify-release.mjs` more carefully for gaps:\n\nThe `verifyReferenced` function checks entries for path, existence, and digest. But it doesn't check:\n- For signatures: that `signature.status` is not null/undefined (current check only catches 'placeholder' and 'missing' strings)\n- For notarization: no status check at all\n\nThe channel check: `channel.artifacts.length !== manifest.artifacts.length` only checks count. Should also verify each manifest artifact path appears in channel artifacts.\n\nLet me now also check: does `buildReleaseManifest` validate that channels have all required fields? Looking at the builder:\n```js\nchannels: channels.map((channel) => ({ ...channel, decision: channel.decision ?? 'BLOCKED', artifacts: normalized.map((artifact) => ({ path: artifact.path, decision: channel.artifactDecisions?.[artifact.path] ?? channel.decision ?? 'BLOCKED' })) })),\n```\n\nThis creates per-artifact decisions. The channel is constructed correctly. But there's no validation that the channel has an `id` field.\n\nAnd in the verifier:\n```js\nfor (const channel of manifest.channels ?? []) if ((channel.artifacts ?? []).length !== (manifest.artifacts ?? []).length) issues.push({ artifact: channel.id ?? 'channel', issue: 'artifact-decision-missing' });\n```\n\nThis only checks count. If a channel has the right number of artifacts but wrong paths, it wouldn't be caught.\n\nOK let me also think about what `buildReleaseManifest` should validate per acceptance criteria: \"Release manifest binds every referenced byte\". The builder should ensure:\n- Every artifact has a non-null path and digest\n- Every referenced file (checksums, sboms, etc.) has a path and digest\n- Version and sourceRevision are present (already checked)\n- Platform is set for native artifacts- Semantic equivalence is specified\n\nNow let me also think about `claims.mjs` \"downgrade mismatches\" more carefully. The current code:\n```js\nconst qualified = Boolean(expected && identity.artifactDigest && identity.corpusDigest && identity.providerDigest && Object.keys(identity).every((key) => identity[key] === expected[key]));\nreturn {\n  state: qualified ? state : 'unproven',\n  qualification: qualified ? identity : null,\n  limitations: item.limitations ?? (qualified ? [] : ['qualification identity incomplete']),\n};\n```\n\nGap: When identity doesn't match expected, the limitations should always include 'qualification identity mismatch' (not just 'qualification identity incomplete'). And when expected is null, it should say 'exact qualification identity absent'. Currently, if the item already has limitations, those are preserved even on mismatch, and the mismatch-specific limitation is lost.\n\nFix: When not qualified, always include the appropriate mismatch limitation. If there are existing item limitations, include them too but ensure the mismatch limitation is present.\n\n```js\nconst identityLimitations = !expected ? ['exact qualification identity absent'] : ['qualification identity mismatch'];\nconst baseLimitations = item.limitations ?? [];\nconst limitations = qualified ? baseLimitations : [...new Set([...baseLimitations, ...identityLimitations])];\n```\n\nActually, I should be more careful. The existing code uses `item.limitations ?? (qualified ? [] : ['qualification identity incomplete'])`. If `item.limitations` is provided (e.g., `['resource-constrained']`), it's used as-is regardless of qualification. This means:\n- If qualified with provided limitations: uses provided limitations ✓\n- If NOT qualified with provided limitations: uses provided limitations, losing the identity mismatch info ✗- If qualified without provided limitations: empty array ✓\n- If NOT qualified without provided limitations: `['qualification identity incomplete']` ✓\n\nThe fix should ensure the identity mismatch limitation is always present when not qualified.\n\nNow let me also look at the `verify-source-provenance.mjs` more carefully for the \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested\" part:\n\n```js\nexport function writeSourceProvenanceQualification(manifest, outputPath, options = {}) {\n  if (!outputPath) throw new OutputError is thrown if no path given. ✓In the CLI:\n```js\nconst outIndex = process.argv.indexOf('--out');\nif (outIndex >= 0) writeFileSync(resolve(process.argv[outIndex + 1]), ...);\n```\n\nThis writes to the file if --out is provided. But `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` is the API function. It always writes when called with an outputPath. The acceptance says \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested\" - I think this is already satisfied because the function only writes when called, and the CLI only provides outputPath when --out is given.\n\nHowever, there's a subtle issue: `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` always writes, even if the result is BLOCKED. The acceptance might mean it should only write when QUALIFIED. But for audit purposes, writing BLOCKED results is useful. I'll leave this as-is since the acceptance says \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested\" which is about the API contract, not about the decision value.\n\nWait, actually re-reading the acceptance more carefully: \"Creator provenance verifies archive/output digests, category mappings, originality, shipped outputs, ownership, transformations, & writes qualification only when explicitly requested.\"\n\nThe \"writes qualification only when explicitly requested\" is about when the write happens. Currently `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` writes whenever called with outputPath. But maybe the function should NOT write by default when called as part of the normal verification flow. Looking at the code, `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` is a separate function from `verifySourceProvenance`. So verification and writing are already separated. The write only happens when explicitly called. This seems correct.\n\nBut wait - does the current code ever write qualification without being explicitly asked? Looking at the CLI, it only writes when --out is provided. Looking at the API, `writeSourceProvenanceQualification` is only called when the caller explicitly wants to write. This is correct.\n\nOK, now let me plan the actual edits:\n\n### B8-017: SBOM/notices reconciliation**sbom.mjs `reconcileDistributionContents`**:\n1. Match shipped components against package contents by name OR by path containment\n2. Add version to completeness check\n3. Add version to the completeness error message\n\n### B8-018: Release manifest binding & verifier\n**release-manifest.mjs `buildReleaseManifest`**:\n1. Validate that each channel has an `id` field\n2. Validate platform is present for native artifacts\n3. Validate semantic equivalent is specified for all artifacts\n\n**verify-release.mjs**:\n1. Fix channel check to verify path-level matching (not just count)\n2. Add null/undefined check for signature.status\n3. Add notarization status check\n4. Check that version is a non-empty string (already checks !manifest.version, but should also check string type)\n\n### B8-024: Claims identity & downgrade\n**claims.mjs**:\n1. Always include identity mismatch limitation when not qualified\n2. Use distinct messages for absent vs mismatched identity\n\n### B8-025: Creator provenance\n**verify-source-provenance.mjs**:\n1. Add shippedOutputs non-emptiness check when source has shipped outputs\n2. Ensure the check is part of the combined condition\n\nLet me also check for the `version` check in sbom.mjs completeness. Currently:\n```js\nif (!component.digest || !component.license || !component.source) missing.push({ kind: 'component-metadata-incomplete', component: component.name });\n```\n\nShould add `!component.version` for non-null version requirement? But the acceptance says \"shipped content & reconcile package contents\" - for SBOM, the version is part of the component metadata. Let me add it.\n\nWait, but version can be null for some component types (external tools, rule material). The inventory function uses `component.version ?? null` which means null is acceptable. For SBOM, the version should be present for library components. I think the version completeness check should be for components that have a `type` of 'library'.\n\nActually, the acceptance criteria says \"reconcile package contents\" - the key thing is that shipped components are present in the package and have complete metadata. For SBOM, having a null version for a shipped library is a gap. Let me add the version check.\n\nLet me also check: does `reconcileDistributionContents` need to handle the case where packageContents are path strings? Currently it does:\n```js\nconst content = new Set(packageContents.map((entry) => typeof entry === 'string' ? entry : entry.name));\n```\n\nThis creates a set of strings. If packageContents are paths like \"lib/index.mjs\", the set would contain those paths. A component named \"nemesis\" wouldn't be found. But a component with path \"lib/index.mjs\" (if stored as name) would be found.\n\nI think the fix should be: for each shipped component, check if any package content entry either equals the component name OR contains the component path. Since components from `inventoryDistribution` have a `name` field but not always a path, and components from `inventoryRuntimeDependencies` have npm package names, the matching should be:\n\n```js\nfunction componentInPackageContents(component, contentSet) {\n  if (contentSet.has(component.name)) return true;\n  // Check if component name appears as a directory or file in the content tree\n  for (const entry of contentSet) {\n    if (entry.startsWith(component.name + '/') || entry === component.name) return true;\n  }\n  return false;\n}\n```\n\nWait, but npm package names like \"@orthic-labs/nemesis\" wouldn't appear as file paths. The package contents would be things like \"lib/index.mjs\", \"bin/nemesis.mjs\", etc. A runtime dependency named \"lodash\" wouldn't be in the file list.\n\nHmm, I think the reconciliation is meant for components that ARE shipped as part of the package, not for npm dependencies. Runtime dependencies are in node_modules and not in the file list. So the reconciliation should focus on creatorMaterial, ruleMaterial, grammars, and externalTools - which are the custom components that would be in the file tree.\n\nBut the function accepts all types. I think the fix should be:\n1. For each shipped component, check if the component name matches any entry in the content set\n2. OR check if any content entry starts with the component name as a prefix (for directory-based components)\n\nLet me simplify: change the matching to do substring or prefix matching:\n\n```js\nconst hasMatch = (component) => content.has(component.name) || [...content].some(entry => entry.startsWith(component.name + '/'));\n```\n\nThis way, a component named \"registry/rules\" would match \"registry/rules/custom.json\".\n\nActually, looking at the package.json `files` array more carefully:\n```json\n\"files\": [\"bin/\", \"lib/\", \"regis