  \"claims\": [],\n  \"knownGaps\": [\"final qualification, provider, corpus, skill, and artifact digests are absent\"]\n}\n```\n\nThis has `qualificationIdentity: null` and no claims. The `knownGaps` message is different from what the code would produce (`'exact qualification identity is absent'`). This suggests the claims.json was manually written or generated by a different version of the code.\n\nThe `support.md` says:\n```\n# Generated support claims\n\nNo public support claim is qualified from current Book 8 evidence. Final qualification, provider, corpus, skill, & artifact digests remain absent.\n```\n\nThis is a human-readable summary that matches the claims.json state.\n\nSince these files are in the allowed list and they represent current state (no qualification identity = no claims), I don't need to change them unless the acceptance criteria requires specific content changes. The current content correctly reflects \"no qualification identity → no claims → known gaps.\" This aligns with the code.\n\nBut wait - the `knownGaps` in claims.json says \"final qualification, provider, corpus, skill, and artifact digests are absent\" while the code would produce \"exact qualification identity is absent\". This discrepancy suggests the claims.json was generated differently. Should I align them?\n\nThe task says \"max 180 changed lines\" and \"smallest corrections.\" Aligning generated file text isn't really a code fix. I'll leave these as-is unless there's a functional issue.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The `claims.json` `knownGaps` says \"final qualification, provider, corpus, skill, and artifact digests are absent\" which is more descriptive than the code's \"exact qualification identity is absent\". The more descriptive version is better for documentation. I'll leave it.\n\nNow, let me also check `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`. It currently says:\n- No runtime dependencies\n- No external engine binaries shipped\n- Rule packs are original material\n- Benchmark fixtures are original material\n\nThis matches the current state (no dependencies, no external engines). The file seems accurate. No changes needed.\n\nAnd `creator-skills.json` - this is the source manifest that gets verified. It currently has placeholder digests and unverified ownership. The verifier correctly produces BLOCKED for this. No changes needed to the data file.\n\nOK, let me finalize my edit plan:\n\n1. **sbom.mjs** (4 lines changed):\n   - Add `inPackage` helper for path-prefix matching\n   - Change `content.has(component.name)` to `inPackage(component)`\n   - Add `!component.version` to completeness check\n\n2. **verify-release.mjs** (~15 lines changed):\n   - Add null guard in artifacts loop\n   - Add null guard + status check in signatures loop\n   - Add null guard + status check in notarizations loop (new)\n   - Fix channel check to use path-level matching\n   - Add null guard in channels loop\n\n3. **claims.mjs** (~3 lines changed):\n   - Fix limitations to always include identity mismatch when not qualified\n\n4. **verify-source-provenance.mjs** (~1 line changed):\n   - Add `!source.shippedOutputs.length` to transformation-proof check\n\nTotal: ~23 lines. Well within 180.\n\nWait, actually I should also look at whether there are issues with the `release-manifest.mjs` builder that could be improved. Let me re-read the builder:\n\n```js\nexport function buildReleaseManifest({ root, version, sourceRevision, artifacts = [], channels = [], checksums = [], sboms = [], notices = [], signatures = [], notarization = [], qualificationArtifacts = [] }) {\n  if (!root || !version || !sourceRevision) throw new Error('root, version, and sourceRevision are required');\n  const normalized = artifacts.map((artifact) => {\n    const path = safeRelativePath(root, artifact.path);\n    const digest = artifact.digest ?? fileDigest(resolve(root, path));\n    if (!digest) throw new Error(`release artifact is missing: ${path}`);\n    return { ...artifact, path, digest };\n  });\n  return {\n    schemaVersion: 1,\n    kind: 'nemesis-release-manifest',\n    version,\n    sourceRevision,\n    artifacts: normalized,\n    checksums: bindRecords(root, checksums), sboms: bindRecords(root, sboms), notices: bindRecords(root, notices),\n    signatures: bindRecords(root, signatures), notarization: bindRecords(root, notarization), qualificationArtifacts: bindRecords(root, qualificationArtifacts),\n    channels: channels.map((channel) => ({ ...channel, decision: channel.decision ?? 'BLOCKED', artifacts: normalized.map((artifact) => ({ path: artifact.path, decision: channel.artifactDecisions?.[artifact.path] ?? channel.decision ?? 'BLOCKED' })) })),\n  };\n}\n```\n\nThe builder binds all bytes (checksums, sboms, notices, signatures, notarization, qualificationArtifacts) via `bindRecords`. Each record gets a path and digest. If the file doesn't exist, `fileDigest` returns null. But `bindRecords` doesn't check for null digests:\n\n```js\nconst bindRecords = (root, records) => records.map((record) => {\n  const path = safeRelativePath(root, record.path);\n  return { ...record, path, digest: record.digest ?? fileDigest(resolve(root, path)) };\n});\n```\n\nIf `fileDigest` returns null (file doesn't exist), the record would have `digest: null`. The verifier would catch this via `verifyReferenced` which checks `!record.digest`. But the builder could also validate this.\n\nHowever, the acceptance criteria says \"Release manifest binds every referenced byte\" - the builder binds bytes by computing digests. If a file is missing, the digest is null, and the verifier catches it. This is the intended flow: the builder doesn't throw on missing files (it records null digest), and the verifier rejects the manifest.\n\nBut wait, for the main artifacts, the builder DOES throw if digest is null:\n```js\nif (!digest) throw new Error(`release artifact is missing: ${path}`);\n```\n\nSo artifacts are strictly validated. But checksums, sboms, notices, etc. are not - they can have null digests. This inconsistency could be a gap. Should the builder also throw for missing supporting files?\n\nI think yes, for consistency. If a release references a checksum file that doesn't exist, that's a build error, not a verification-time error. Let me add a check in `bindRecords`:\n\n```js\nconst bindRecords = (root, records) => records.map((record) => {\n  const path = safeRelativePath(root, record.path);\n  const digest = record.digest ?? fileDigest(resolve(root, path));\n  if (!digest) throw new Error(`release reference ${record.path} is missing or empty`);\n  return { ...record, path, digest };\n});\n```\n\nThis makes the builder fail-fast on missing supporting files. But this is a behavioral change - currently the builder would create a manifest with null digests, and the verifier would reject it. With this change, the builder would throw during creation.\n\nHmm, the acceptance says \"verifier rejects\" not \"builder rejects.\" So the builder should create the manifest (even with null digests) and the verifier should reject it. Let me NOT add this validation to the builder.\n\nActually wait, the acceptance says \"Release manifest binds every referenced byte\" - this means the builder should bind (compute digest for) every referenced byte. If a byte is missing, it can't be bound. The builder currently tries to bind (via `fileDigest`) but doesn't fail if the result is null. \"Binds every referenced byte\" implies that every reference should resolve to an actual byte. So the builder should fail if a referenced byte is missing.\n\nLet me add the validation:\n\n```js\nconst bindRecords = (root, records) => records.map((record) => {\n  const path = safeRelativePath(root, record.path);\n  const digest = record.digest ?? fileDigest(resolve(root, path));\n  if (!digest) throw new Error(`release reference is missing: ${record.path}`);\n  return { ...record, path, digest };\n});\n```\n\nThis is a small change (2 lines added) and ensures the builder fails if any referenced file is missing.\n\nOK, let me finalize the edit list:\n\n1. **release-manifest.mjs** (2 lines): Add null-digest check in `bindRecords`\n2. **sbom.mjs** (4 lines): Path-prefix matching + version check\n3. **verify-release.mjs** (~15 lines): Null guards, status checks, path matching\n4. **claims.mjs** (~3 lines): Identity mismatch limitations\n5. **verify-source-provenance.mjs** (~1 line): shippedOutputs.length check\n\nTotal: ~25 lines. Well within 180.\n\nLet me now write the actual patches.\n\nActually, let me re-examine the verify-release.mjs edits more carefully. The current signature check:\n\n```js\nfor (const signature of manifest.signatures ?? []) if (!signature.digest || ['placeholder', 'missing'].includes(signature.status)) issues.push({ artifact: signature.path ?? 'signature', issue: 'placeholder-signature' });\n```\n\nIssues:\n1. If `signature` is null → accessing `.digest` throws2. If `signature.status` is null/undefined → `includes(null)` is false, so placeholder with null status passes\n3. No check for empty string status\n\nFix:\n```js\nfor (const signature of manifest.signatures ?? []) if (!signature || !signature.digest || !signature.status || ['placeholder', 'missing'].includes(signature.status)) issues.push({ artifact: signature?.path ?? 'signature', issue: 'placeholder-signature' });\n```\n\nThe `!signature.status` catches null, undefined, empty string. The `!signature` catches null entries.\n\nFor notarization, there's no explicit loop. I need to add one:\n```js\nfor (const entry of manifest.notarization ?? []) if (!entry || !entry.digest || !entry.status || ['placeholder', 'missing'].includes(entry.status)) issues.push({ artifact: entry?.path ?? 'notarization', issue: 'notarization-gap' });\n```\n\nWait, but `verifyReferenced` already checks notarization entries for path and digest. The additional check is for status. Let me add the status check after the existing `verifyReferenced` call for notarization:\n\nActually, looking at the code structure, the signature status check is done in a separate loop after `verifyReferenced`. The notarization doesn't have a similar status check. I should add one. But to keep it consistent with the signature pattern, I'll add it after the signature loop:\n\n```js\nfor (const entry of manifest.notarization ?? []) if (!entry || !entry.digest || !entry.status || ['placeholder', 'missing'].includes(entry.status)) issues.push({ artifact: entry?.path ?? 'notarization', issue: 'notarization-gap' });\n```\n\nFor the channel path matching:\n```js\n// Current:\nfor (const channel of manifest.channels ?? []) if ((channel.artifacts ?? []).length !== (manifest.artifacts ?? []).length) issues.push({ artifact: channel.id ?? 'channel', issue: 'artifact-decision-missing' });\n\n// Fixed:\nfor (const channel of manifest.channels ?? []) {\n  if (!channel) { issues.push({ artifact: 'channel', issue: 'null-entry' }); continue; }\n  const channelPaths = new Set((channel.artifacts ?? []).map((a) => a.path));\n  if ((channel.artifacts ?? []).length !== (manifest.artifacts ?? []).length || (manifest.artifacts ?? []).some((a) => !channelPaths.has(a.path))) issues.push({ artifact: channel.id ?? 'channel', issue: 'artifact-decision-missing' });\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me simplify. The key fix is to check path-level matching instead of just count. But I also need the null guard. Let me write it as:\n\n```js\nfor (const channel of manifest.channels ?? []) {\n  if (!channel) { issues.push({ artifact: 'channel', issue: 'null-entry' }); continue; }\n  const channelPaths = new Set((channel.artifacts ?? []).map((a) => a.path));\n  const missingPath = (manifest.artifacts ?? []).some((a) => !channelPaths.has(a.path));\n  if (missingPath || (channel.artifacts ?? []).length !== (manifest.artifacts ?? []).length) issues.push({ artifact: channel.id ?? 'channel', issue: 'artifact-decision-missing' });\n}\n```\n\nThis replaces the single-line for loop with a block. Let me count lines: original was 1 line, new is 7 lines. Net change: +6 lines.\n\nActually, I can make it more compact:\n```js\nfor (const channel of manifest.channels ?? []) if (!channel) issues.push({ artifact: 'channel', issue: 'null-entry' }); else { const paths = new Set((channel.artifacts ?? []).map((a) => a.path)); if ((manifest.artifacts ?? []).some((a) => !paths.has(a.path))) issues.push({ artifact: channel.id ?? 'channel', issue: 'artifact-decision-missing' }); }\n```\n\nThat's 3 lines (or 1 long line). Let me use the multi-line format for readability.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The current check is:\n```js\nif ((channel.artifacts ?? []).length !== (manifest.artifacts ?? []).length)\n```\n\nThis catches count mismatches. But it misses the case where count matches but paths differ. The fix should be:\n```js\nconst channelPaths = new Set((channel.artifacts ?? []).map((a) => a.path));\nconst missingPath = (manifest.artifacts ?? []).some((a) => !channelPaths.has(a.path));\n```\n\nIf `missingPath` is true, there's a path mismatch. If count also mismatches, there's also a count mismatch. Either way, it's an `artifact-decision-missing` issue.\n\nLet me write the replacement more carefully. The original is a single `for...if` statement. I'll replace it with a block:\n\nOriginal:\n```js\n  for (const channel of manifest.channels ?? []) if ((channel.artifacts ?? []).length !== (manifest.artifacts ?? []).length) issues.push({ artifact: channel.id ?? 'channel', issue: 'artifact-decision-missing' });\n```\n\nReplacement:\n```js\n  for (const channel of manifest.channels ?? []) {\n    if (!channel) { issues.push({ artifact: 'channel', issue: 'null-entry' }); continue; }\n    const channelPaths = new Set((channel.artifacts ?? []).map((a) => a.path));\n    if ((manifest.artifacts ?? []).some((a) => !channelPaths.has(a.path))) issues.push({ artifact: channel.id ?? 'channel', issue: 'artifact-decision-missing' });\n  }\n```\n\nThis adds null guard and path-level matching.5 lines replacing 1 line, net +4 lines.\n\nNow let me also think about the artifacts loop null guard. Current:\n```js\n for (const artifact of manifest.artifacts ?? []) {\n    if (artifact.type === 'native' && !artifact.platform) issues.push({ artifact: artifact.path, issue: 'platform-missing' });\n    if (artifact.semanticEquivalent === false) issues.push({ artifact: artifact.path, issue: 'semantic-equivalence-failed' });\n  }\n```\n\nIf `artifact` is null, accessing `.type` throws. Fix:\n```js\n  for (const artifact of manifest.artifacts ?? []) {\n    if (!artifact) { issues.push({ artifact: 'artifact', issue: 'null-entry' }); continue; }\n    if (artifact.type === 'native' && !artifact.platform) issues.push({ artifact: artifact.path, issue: 'platform-missing' });\n    if (artifact.semanticEquivalent === false) issues.push({ artifact: artifact.path, issue: 'semantic-equivalence-failed' });\n  }\n```\n\n+2 lines.\n\nOK, let me now write all the patches. Let me also double-check the verify-release.mjs structure to make sure I'm inserting in the right place.\n\nLooking at verify-release.mjs structure:\n1. sha256File function\n2. resolvedArtifact function\n3. verifyReferenced function\n4. verifyReleaseManifest function\n5. verifyReleaseManifestObject function (the main logic)\n6. CLI block\n\nThe changes are all in `verifyReleaseManifestObject`. Let me identify the exact lines:\n\nLine 49-51: for loop checking checksums/sboms/notices/signatures/notarization/qualificationArtifacts\nLine 53-56: SBOM validation loop\nLine 58: Signature status check\nLine 59-62: Artifact platform/equivalence check\nLine 63: Channel count check\nLine 64-65: Version check and return\n\nWait, let me re-count. The file is 85 lines. Let me look at the structure again:\n\n```\n60:   for (const signature of manifest.signatures ?? []) if (!signature.digest || ['placeholder', 'missing'].includes(signature.status)) issues.push({ artifact: signature.path ?? 'signature', issue: 'placeholder-signature' });\n61:   for (const artifact of manifest.artifacts ?? []) {\n62:     if (artifact.type === 'native' && !artifact.platform) issues.push({ artifact: artifact.path, issue: 'platform-missing' });\n63:     if (artifact.semanticEquivalent === false) issues.push({ artifact: artifact.path, issue: 'semantic-equivalence-failed' });\n64:   }\n65:   for (const channel of manifest.channels ?? []) if ((channel.artifacts ?? []).length !== (manifest.artifacts ?? []).length) issues.push({ artifact: channel.id ?? 'channel', issue: 'artifact-decision-missing' });\n66:   if (!manifest.version || !manifest.sourceRevision) issues.push({ artifact: 'manifest', issue: 'identity-missing' });\n```\n\nOK so I need to:\n1. Fix line 60 (signature check) → add null guard + status check\n2. Add notarization status check after line 60\n3. Fix lines 61-64 (artifact loop) → add null guard\n4. Fix line 65 (channel check) → add path matching + null guard\n\nLet me now write the actual apply_patch commands. I need to be very precise with the patch format.\n\nLet me re-read the file content one more time to make sure I have the exact text:"}}
